          "text": "**Clinical Indication**  \nFocal seizures with impaired awareness, onset at age 38, occurring approximately 5 times per month. Etiology is categorized as genetic. Seizures are currently drug-responsive. Current antiseizure medications: topiramate and oxcarbazepine.\n\n**Technique**  \nRoutine scalp EEG recorded using the international 10–20 electrode system with standard referential and bipolar montages. The recording was reviewed for background activity, interictal epileptiform discharges, and electrographic seizures.\n\n**Findings**  \nNo specific interictal or ictal EEG findings are provided for interpretation in the available record. Accordingly, no EEG-based conclusion regarding epileptiform activity or seizure localization can be made.\n\n**Impression**  \nEEG interpretation is limited because waveform findings are not available. The clinical history is of focal seizures with impaired awareness, with onset at age 38, approximately 5 episodes per month, genetic etiology, and current drug-responsive status while taking topiramate and oxcarbazepine.",
